[12:12:08] scan
  SPY: passed on 6 candidates — Came closest to the 2026-10-16 737/733 put credit spread — top score 59.4, 20-delta short 4.4% OTM, clean $357 max loss. What stopped it is the friction arithmetic at the one-lot cap: $43 of max gain against ~$15 round-trip cost (2 legs x $7.50) plus $4.50 of entry slippage, so even a perfect hold-to-expiry outcome nets ~$23, and a realistic 50-70% early close nets ~$5-10 against $357 of tail. That is roughly 45% of the theoretical edge handed to execution before the market does anything, and the reward_risk sub-score of 0.024 is the same fact in another font. The 737/735 sibling is strictly worse ($23 gain, same friction). Second-closest was the 800/805 call credit spread, which is the only candidate whose economics scale sensibly ($96 gain, $1.50 slippage, 0.5% quoted width, 297 lots/day) — but it carries bias_fit 0.0 against the desk's mild bullish read into an NVDA-bid tape, and its own POP math (0.797 x 96 vs 0.203 x 404) is negative before costs, so I am not paying to be short upside here. Both iron condors are non-starters: short 771 put / 779 call straddles spot at 770.555, 0.125-0.158 POP, and $13.50-$17.00 of slippage across four thin legs. No position, no SPY slot consumed, nothing lost by waiting for a strike set where the credit covers its own exit.
